# 17.10 Design Decisions

## Why Flat Folders?

A flat hierarchy:

- Simplifies queries
- Eliminates recursive relationships
- Reduces implementation complexity
- Improves frontend usability

Nested folders may be introduced in future versions.

---

## Why System Folders?

Core application features rely on predictable folder locations.

Examples:

- Uploaded
- Quick Notes
- Archive

Managing these as system folders simplifies backend logic while maintaining a consistent user experience.

---

## Why folder_type ENUM?

Business logic depends on folder behavior rather than folder names.

Using an ENUM:

- Prevents invalid folder categories
- Supports localization
- Avoids hardcoded string comparisons
- Simplifies backend routing

---

## Why Partial Unique Index?

The partial unique index guarantees that every user has exactly one instance of each required system folder without restricting the number of custom folders.

---

## Why Store document_count?

The `document_count` field is maintained as a cached value to avoid expensive aggregate queries every time the Document Library is rendered.

This improves UI responsiveness, especially as document collections grow.

---

# 17.11 Business Rules

- Every folder belongs to exactly one authenticated user.
- Every document belongs to exactly one folder.
- Users may create unlimited custom folders.
- Users cannot create additional system folders.
- Users cannot delete system folders.
- Users cannot modify the folder_type of system folders.
- Users may rename only custom folders.
- Users may move documents between folders at any time.
- Deleting a custom folder never deletes its documents.
- Documents from deleted folders are automatically moved to the Uploaded folder unless another destination is selected.
- Only one Uploaded folder may exist per user.
- Only one Quick Notes folder may exist per user.
- Only one Archive folder may exist per user.
- Archived documents remain searchable through the AI retrieval pipeline.
- Recently Deleted documents are excluded from all retrieval operations.

---

# 17.12 Future Expansion

Future versions may introduce:

- Nested folders
- Folder sharing
- Folder permissions
- Folder color customization
- Custom folder icons
- Folder pinning
- Folder favorites
- Smart folders
- AI-generated folder suggestions
- Team workspaces

The current schema supports these additions without structural redesign.

---

# 17.13 Summary

The `folders` table provides a scalable and maintainable organizational layer for KnowledgeOS.

By separating system folders from user-created folders, enforcing a flat hierarchy, utilizing a dedicated `folder_type` enumeration, and guaranteeing folder uniqueness through partial indexes, the design remains simple for the MVP while providing a strong foundation for future expansion.

This architecture enables intuitive document organization, efficient querying, and seamless integration with the AI retrieval pipeline without introducing unnecessary relational complexity.

